## Purpose
Tests the ability to implement offline-first, real-time collaboration with a patch-based DOM updater and proper sequencing. Covers DOM operations, fetch, WebSocket handling, Service Worker caching, and error handling with reconnection strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Patch-based DOM updates using small, immutable operations
- Per-cell sequencing and out-of-order patch buffering
- Offline-first architecture with Service Worker stale-while-revalidate
- Reconnection strategy with exponential backoff and jitter

## Code Example
```javascript
// REST/WS wrapper with reconnection
class GridClient {
  constructor(url) { this.url = url; this.ws = null; this.seq = {}; }
  async init() {
    const layout = await fetch(this.url + '/grid').then(r => r.json());
    this.render(layout);
    this.connect();
  }
  connect() {
    this.ws = new WebSocket(this.url.replace('http','ws') + '/stream');
    this.ws.onmessage = e => this.process(JSON.parse(e.data));
    this.ws.onclose = () => setTimeout(() => this.connect(), this.backoff());
  }
  backoff() { return Math.min(30000, (this.attempts || 1) * 1000 * (0.5 + Math.random())); }

  // Patch processor with buffering
  process(patch) {
    const { cellId, seq } = patch;
    if (!this.seq[cellId] || seq > this.seq[cellId]) this.pending[cellId]?.push(patch);
    while (this.pending[cellId]?.length) {
      const p = this.pending[cellId].sort((a,b) => a.seq - b.seq).shift();
      if (p.seq === this.seq[cellId] + 1) { this.apply(p); this.seq[cellId] = p.seq; }
    }
  }
  apply({cellId, op, value}) {
    const el = document.getElementById(cellId);
    if (op === 'set') el.textContent = value;
    else if (op === 'add') el.insertAdjacentHTML('beforeend', value);
    else if (op === 'del') el.remove();
  }
}
```

## Concept Overview
Dynamic programming optimizes recursive problems by caching subproblem results. In React, useMemo prevents expensive recalculations during re-renders.

## Implementation Details
### Top-Down Memoization (Recursive)
```javascript
const fibonacci = useMemo(() => {
  const memo = {};
  const fib = (n) => {
    if (n in memo) return memo[n];
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    memo[n] = fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
    return memo[n];
  };
  return fib;
}, []);
```

### Bottom-Up Tabulation (Iterative)
```javascript
const fibonacci = useMemo(() => {
  return (n) => {
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    let prev = 0, curr = 1;
    for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
      [prev, curr] = [curr, prev + curr];
    }
    return curr;
  };
}, []);
```

## Common Pitfalls
- **Dependency array**: Empty array [] ensures function isn't recreated
- **Memory leaks**: Large memo objects can cause memory issues
- **Over-optimization**: useMemo overhead may exceed benefits for simple calculations
- **Referential equality**: useMemo preserves function reference for child components

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical streaming data handling in the browser: async iteration, backpressure management, error handling, and cancellation. It mirrors real-world applications that consume large event streams, logs, or real-time data feeds.

## Key Concepts

- **Fetch streaming responses** and ReadableStream APIs
- **NDJSON parsing** with line-by-line buffering
- **AsyncIterator** / for await...of syntax
- **Backpressure** with bounded queues (max 5 items)
- **AbortController** for cancellation and proper error propagation
- **Closures** to encapsulate internal state (buffer, reader, etc.)

## Code Example

```javascript
function createStreamingFetcher(url) {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const buffer = [];
  let resolveNext;
  let rejectNext;

  const queue = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    resolveNext = resolve;
    rejectNext = reject;
  });

  async function start() {
    const response = await fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal });
    const reader = response.body.getReader();
    const decoder = new TextDecoder();
    let buffer = '';

    while (true) {
      const { done, value } = await reader.read();
      if (done) {
        if (buffer.trim()) {
          try {
            resolveNext(JSON.parse(buffer));
          } catch (e) {
            rejectNext(new Error('Invalid NDJSON'));
          }
        }
        break;
      }

      buffer += decoder.decode(value, { stream: true });
      const lines = buffer.split('\n');
      buffer = lines.pop();

      for (const line of lines) {
        if (line.trim()) {
          try {
            resolveNext(JSON.parse(line));
          } catch (e) {
            rejectNext(new Error('Invalid NDJSON'));
          }
          if (buffer.length >= 5) {
            await queue;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  start().catch(e => rejectNext(e));

  return {
    stream: {
      [Symbol.asyncIterator]() {
        return {
          next() {
            return queue.then(v => ({ value: v, done: false }));
          }
        };
      }
    },
    cancel: () => controller.abort()
  };
}
```

